"Can a faithful normal state always be defined on a C*-algebra[0m[38;2;124;124;124macting on a non-separable Hilbert space?"[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully. The question is about whether a faithful [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnormal state can always be defined on a C*-algebra acting on a non-separable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHilbert space.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me check for answer leaks. The problem statement is just the question[0m[38;2;124;124;124mitself, no solutions or hints. No leak detected.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let me think about the mathematics.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA C*-algebra acting on a Hilbert space H means a C*-subalgebra of B(H) (bounded [0m[38;2;124;124;124moperators on H).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA state on a C*-algebra A is a positive linear functional of norm 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA state is faithful if φ(a*a) = 0 implies a = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA state is normal if it is weak*-continuous on bounded parts, or equivalently, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mit can be represented as φ(a) = Tr(ρa) for some positive trace-class operator ρ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwith Tr(ρ) = 1 (when A ⊆ B(H)).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, more precisely: a normal state on B(H) corresponds to a density operator [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(positive trace-class operator with trace 1). A normal state on a C*-subalgebra [0m[38;2;124;124;124mA ⊆ B(H) is the restriction of a normal state on B(H) (by normality, it [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mextends).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me be more careful. A state φ on A ⊆ B(H) is normal if it is σ-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mweakly continuous. Normal states on B(H) are exactly those of the form φ(a) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mTr(ρa) where ρ is a positive trace-class operator with Tr(ρ) = 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a normal state to be faithful on A, we need: if a ∈ A, a ≥ 0, and Tr(ρa) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0, then a = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the question is about non-separable Hilbert spaces.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey fact: A trace-class operator on any Hilbert space has separable range. This [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis because a compact operator (and trace-class operators are compact) has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseparable range. More precisely, the range of a compact operator is separable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(its closure is separable, and actually the range itself is separable since [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact operators map bounded sets to relatively compact sets, and compact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmetric spaces are separable).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me think again. A trace-class operator ρ is compact. The range of a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcompact operator on a Hilbert space is separable. Actually, the closure of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrange is separable. The range itself: a compact operator has separable range [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbecause the image of the unit ball is relatively compact, hence separable, and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe range is the union of n times the image of the unit ball, which is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcountable union of separable sets, hence separable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo ρ has separable range. Let's call the closure of the range K, which is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseparable subspace.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, if H is non-separable, there exists a non-zero vector ξ orthogonal to K [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(the closure of the range of ρ). Consider the rank-one projection p = |ξ⟩⟨ξ| [0m
[38;2;124;124;124monto the span of ξ. Then ρξ = 0 (since ξ ⊥ range(ρ), and ρ is self-adjoint so [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mker(ρ) = (range(ρ))^⊥). So Tr(ρp) = ⟨ρξ, ξ⟩ = 0 (since ρξ = 0). But p ≠ 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if p ∈ A (the C*-algebra), then φ(p) = Tr(ρp) = 0 but p ≠ 0, so φ is not [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfaithful.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut the question is: can we always define a faithful normal state? The answer [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdepends on whether A contains such a projection.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the question is whether it's ALWAYS possible. So we need to find a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcounterexample: a C*-algebra acting on a non-separable Hilbert space that admits[0m[38;2;124;124;124mno faithful normal state.[0m

